('a6d00ed5-bf24-445f-93e0-0b5c2fe8c840', 'f90fc22c-a15d-439e-8fde-5155de2762bf', 'assistant', '<think>\nAlright, let\'s break down how I approached creating the motion design for the \"Hello World\" project.\n\nFirst, I considered the user\'s request for a simple motion design. This meant keeping the elements minimal and the animations straightforward. The primary focus was on the text \"Hello World,\" so I decided to center it on the screen for maximum visibility.\n\nNext, I thought about the structure. Using HyperFrames, I knew I needed a root index.html file to set the total duration. Since the user wanted a 5-second video, I set data-duration to 5.\n\nFor the composition, I created an intro scene in compositions/intro.html. Centering the text was achieved using Flexbox properties in CSS, ensuring it looked clean and professional. Choosing the Arial font was a deliberate choice for its readability and simplicity.\n\nWhen it came to animations, I wanted to add a welcoming feel without overcomplicating things. I used CSS keyframes to create an entrance animation where the text fades in and scales up. This gives a subtle yet effective introduction. To add a bit of dynamic interest, I included a bounce effect on the \'o\' in \"Hello.\" This small touch makes the animation feel more alive without being distracting.\n\nThe exit animation was designed to smoothly transition', NULL, NULL, '2026-06-14 18:44:09'),
('6ac00d66-3504-4e2f-9ecb-d52b3335e2df', '86b7e652-6c2e-4a50-870b-2cbeb3d2bf5c', 'user', 'cree moi un motion design simple hello world en texte', NULL, NULL, '2026-06-14 18:51:15'),
('a536b5e8-84af-4015-bb3c-ca5c84eccd00', '86b7e652-6c2e-4a50-870b-2cbeb3d2bf5c', 'assistant', 'Generation completed with some errors. Check the task list for details.', 'Okay, so the user wants me to create a motion design for a \"Hello World\" text. They mentioned using HyperFrames HTML rules, so I need to make sure I follow those strictly.\n\nFirst, I\'ll start by setting up the project structure. The project name is \"hello-world\", which is a kebab slug, so that\'s good. The title is straightforward, and the description is a one-sentence summary.\n\nFor the visual identity, I need to choose colors that contrast well. I\'ll go with a primary color of #ffffff for the text, a dark background #1a1a1a, and the text color as #ffffff to ensure readability.\n\nNext, I\'ll structure the HTML. The root div must have id=\"root\" and data-duration. I\'ll set the duration to 5 seconds since the animation is simple. Inside this div, I\'ll create a div with class=\"clip\" for the animated element. The text \"Hello World\" will be inside a <h1> tag for better styling.\n\nNow, onto the CSS. I\'ll define @keyframes for opacity and transform to create a fade-in and slide-up effect. The animation will start at 1 second and last for 3 seconds. I\'ll apply these animations to the .clip element using the animation property.\n\nI\'ll also set up the body to have a black background and center the content using flexbox. The text will have a white color to stand out against the background.\n\nFinally, I\'ll write the JSON response, making sure to include all the necessary tasks: creating the project, writing the index.html file, and rendering the output.mp4. I\'ll ensure that the HTML content is correctly formatted within the JSON object.\n\nI think that covers everything. The result should be a simple, elegant motion design that meets all the specified requirements.', NULL, '2026-06-14 18:52:50'),
