('936c879c-08d8-43ef-88c4-4cc86199ff0f', 'bf9c500d-55ef-4a8a-a979-85c73620d2ae', 'assistant', 'Motion design generated successfully. 3 tasks completed.', 'Okay, I need to figure out how to respond to the user\'s request. They want a motion design with three scenes, using yellow as the base color. Let me break down what I need to do.\n\nFirst, I remember the structure I need to follow. The user provided a specific JSON format, so I have to make sure I stick to that. The action is \"create_motion\", and I need to set the project name, title, and description.\n\nFor the visual identity, the primary color is yellow, so I\'ll use #FFD700. The background should be a dark color to make the yellow stand out, maybe #111, and the text color can be white for contrast.\n\nNext, the tasks. I need to initialize the project, write the HTML file, and render the video. The HTML should include three scenes. Each scene will be a clip with specific start times and durations.\n\nI\'ll structure each scene with a div that has the class \"clip\" and the necessary data attributes. The first scene will have a large \"Hello\" text, the second \"World\" a bit smaller, and the third a greeting in another language, maybe \"Bonjour Monde\" in French. Each text will fade in at different times to create a smooth transition.\n\nI\'ll make sure each clip has an animation using @keyframes fadeIn. The animations should run forwards so the text stays visible after the fade-in. I\'ll set the start times so each scene appears after the previous one has finished its animation.\n\nI need to ensure the HTML is self-contained and follows the HyperFrames template exactly. The body should be 1920x1080 with a black background. Each clip will be centered and styled with the appropriate colors and font sizes.\n\nFinally, I\'ll compile all this into the JSON structure, making sure there\'s no extra text or markdown. The response should only be the JSON object with the correct parameters and HTML content.', NULL, '